  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 229 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nSaturday throughout Tuesday, the upper level ridge starts to \nweaken over the weekend and plateau over the Central and \nNorthern Plains before sliding to the east at the end of the \nperiod. There is some uncertainty within the guidance as the low\npressure in the Northwest Pacific coast starts to move east \ncausing some inconsistencies within the models. Although, this \nwill help temperatures continue to be above normal and bring in \npotential chances for rain later in the period as embedded \nshortwave troughs become more prevalent. High confidence (70-80%) \nthat temperatures will start in the mid to upper 90s Saturday and \ndrop into the mid 90s Sunday. Confidence drops to low (30%) on \nMonday as NBM 75th to 25th percentile temperature spread becomes \nmore than 10 degrees over the northern half of the County Warning \nArea (CWA). This could see potential highs within the low 90s for \nboth Monday and Tuesday due to possible showers.\n\nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) for showers later in the \nperiod has increased overnight due to the low pressure potentially\nmoving east with continuing upper level southwesterly flow and \ndiurnal heating still being the main driving force. There is high \nconfidence (70%) for showers on Saturday as not much change has \nhappened with it still having relatively low chances being around \n20% within the majority of the CWA. However, there is low to \nmedium confidence (25-40%) that showers could occur between Sunday\nnight and Tuesday night as there is inconsistency within the \nupper level pattern and embedded shortwave troughs passing through.\nOverall, chances for showers to occur will be around 30% in the \nnorthwestern half of the CWA and 20% in the southeastern half.",
